To avoid a format-overflow warning when using `sprintf`, you need to ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the formatted output. For example, if your original code looks like this:

```c
void f (int a, int b)
{
  char buf [13];
  sprintf (buf, "a = %i, b = %i\n", a, b);
}
```
You can increase the buffer size to at least 14 bytes:

```c
void f (int a, int b)
{
  char buf [14];
  sprintf (buf, "a = %i, b = %i\n", a, b);
}
```
The warning is triggered because the buffer might not be large enough to hold the null terminator. Increasing its size by one byte ensures there's room for it.

